<!--
  Stage 15: what the managed linker must not remove from a release player.

  The WebGL release candidate runs with Managed Stripping Level = High and IL2CPP
  code generation = OptimizeSize (the `optimize-size` profile, ADR 0011 /
  docs/build/size-baseline.md). Stripping is what makes the player small; it is
  also the one setting that can remove a type nothing *statically* references
  but the runtime looks up by name. This file is the allow-list for exactly the
  assemblies where that can happen, and every entry names why.

  Rules for editing this file:
    * one assembly per block, with the reason in the comment above it;
    * a new entry needs evidence (a failing release player, or code that resolves
      a type by name), not caution — `preserve="all"` costs bytes in every build;
    * the five packages named by the Stage 15 brief are the five reflection paths
      the game actually reaches: Serilog-less config tables (Luban), the save
      codecs (MessagePack), the external contract probe (Google.Protobuf), the
      container (VContainer) and the UI state streams (R3).

  The release gate that proves this list is enough is the browser suite run
  against the *Release* player (`tools/test.ps1 -Suite Full -Release`): boot's
  seven probes include the MessagePack and Protobuf round trips and the DI
  resolution, and the persistence spec saves and reloads a real run through
  IndexedDB, which is the path that deserialises a checkpoint.
-->
<linker>

  <!--
    Luban.Runtime - `StringUtil.ToString` walks an object's fields and properties
    with reflection (`GetType().GetFields()`); the generated table rows are passed
    to it when a config error is reported. It is diagnostics-only, but a stripped
    copy would turn a config failure into a second failure while explaining the
    first.
  -->
  <assembly fullname="Luban.Runtime" preserve="all" />

  <!--
    MessagePack - the formatters for the save DTOs are generated into
    ProjectSurvive.Core.Contracts (source generator, direct references), but the
    serializer resolves them through the resolver chain at runtime, and
    `MessagePack.Annotations` holds the attributes that chain reads. Preserve the
    runtime assembly rather than the generated one: the generated resolver is a
    static reference from the codec, so it is not at risk.
  -->
  <assembly fullname="MessagePack" preserve="all" />
  <assembly fullname="MessagePack.Annotations" preserve="all" />

  <!--
    Google.Protobuf - the generated `AppInfo` message is direct code, but
    `FileDescriptor.FromGeneratedCode` builds descriptors by reflection over the
    descriptor pool, which is what `AppInfo.Descriptor` touches at first use. Only
    the external-contract probe uses it.
  -->
  <assembly fullname="Google.Protobuf" preserve="all" />

  <!--
    VContainer - registrations that name a type are resolved through constructors
    found by reflection. The project registers with explicit factories (see
    llmwiki/gotchas/il2cpp-di-and-reflection.md), so the resolution itself is not
    reflection, but the container still reflects over registered types to build
    its resolution cache and to format its own diagnostics.
  -->
  <assembly fullname="VContainer" preserve="all" />

  <!--
    R3 - subscription plumbing is generic code, but `ObservableTracker`/operator
    diagnostics and the `[Preserve]`-driven observer types are reached through
    reflection-free but generic instantiations that the linker cannot always see.
    Keeping the assembly costs little and removes the "works in the Editor, throws
    in the player" class of failure from the UI state layer.
  -->
  <assembly fullname="R3" preserve="all" />

</linker>
